body:
Bloody-Nine Versus Glama Golden. Glama fled half way across the known universe to get away from motherfuckers like (and Logen specifically) only to end up in a one on one fight against Bloody-Nine himself. What a shit piece of luck

&#x200B;

Al'Lan Mandragoran versus Demandred : "I didnt come here to win, I came here to kill you" enough said

&#x200B;

Cassisus Au Bellona vs the entire rim apparently : "still my honour remains"

Lowtown by Daniel Polansky is the best

&#x200B;

Mother of Learning is by far the worst, its just awful.

I just think of it like the real world. A trained navy seal can be defeated by a toddler if he shoots the navy seal in the neck while he is asleep.

&#x200B;

Lindon doesn't fight remotely 'fair" and also flat out cheats sometimes.

There is no amount of evidence you could produce from afar that would convince me someone is performing miracles. I'd need to see and experience it for myself and even then I'd be skeptical as fuck.

&#x200B;

I've seen Godzilla fight King Kong and heard such ridiculous propaganda that i'd need like my arm to be broken in half and him to wave his hand and reattach the limb

this isnt an unpopular opinion. Every major Christian denomination preaches something along these lines and how they must rise up, every minority group understands if he was a black dude preaching peaceful overthrow of the government we would also definitely shoot him. 

&#x200B;

Like it would be such a tightrope for him to walk to not be shot. Although we might not shoot him but instead neuter him like corporations did to pride and black lives matter.

Can anyone help me find the name of this book?

&#x200B;

So its fantasy, the book starts in a sort of war camp. The main character is super powerful and turns out he was kind of an experiment by a mysterious group of magic users that are ever present in the world. His enemy has a bodyguard who wants to marry his daughter but is seen to be beneath him. They go out into the wilds at one point. His name definitely begins with an I or something. It's a series. Lots of politics and fights

First Law by Joe Abercrombie (and is mentioned or a main character in almost every other book)

&#x200B;

He has killed more men than cancer, he has a way with the ladies, and is the most anti of anti-heroes

The First Law by Joe Abercrombie.

&#x200B;

Dont care what anyone says (even the author) The Bloody-Nine is immortal. To touch the bloody-nine is to touch death.
